<title>JAPAN: Japan parties favour public money for depositors.</title>
<headline>Japan parties favour public money for depositors.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1997-03-03</dateline>
<text>
<p>Ruling and opposition parties share the view that public funds must be used to protect depositors when financial institutions collapse, senior officials of the parties said in a television programme broadcast over the weekend.</p>
<p>&quot;It cannot be helped if banks go bankrupt. But we will see panic if depositors cannot get their money back,&quot; said Koichi Kato, secretary-general of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party.  </p>
<p>Takeo Nishioka, secretary-general of Japan's main opposition group, the New Frontier Party, said the present deposit insurance system could not cope with bank failures.</p>
<p>&quot;If banks were to disclose further information, some of them may turn out to be in serious shape,&quot; Nishioka said.</p>
</text>
